1
00:00:01,180 --> 00:00:04,100
Hello everyone, and welcome
to a two-part lecture

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,460
series on laboratory
medicine and anemias. My name

3
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:10,240
is Noah Chernick, and
before we get started with

4
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:12,720
the lectures, I want to
quickly thank Dr. Saggio for

5
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:15,020
all of his guidance in
developing these lectures.

6
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:18,840
I'm available to address
any questions, concerns,

7
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:21,960
or comments that you
may have in regards to

8
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:25,400
this lecture or anything
anemia-related. Please

9
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:27,520
reach out if you want
to meet one-on-one or

10
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:30,000
in a group setting. either
are completely fine.

11
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:33,660
Here are our learning
objectives for today's

12
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:36,440
lecture, both for
part 1 and for part 2.

13
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:41,140
And for our lecture
layout today, part 1

14
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:43,460
will again just dive
into the general

15
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:46,520
concepts of anemia
and microcytic anemia.

16
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:48,860
Part 2 will deal with the

17
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:51,140
normocytic and
macrocytic anemias.

18
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:55,980
So before we discuss
specific microcytic

19
00:00:55,980 --> 00:00:58,100
anemias, I want to
quickly talk about some

20
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:00,680
of the the basics of
underlying anemias.

21
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:05,780
So, very simply, anemia
is a significant deficit

22
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:08,840
or decrease in the mass
of circulating red blood

23
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:11,960
cells. But why do we
care about this decrease?

24
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:14,400
Red blood cells, as
most of you should know,

25
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:17,360
transport oxygen to cells
and tissues. If we have a

26
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:20,040
decrease of the function
or number of red blood

27
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:23,000
cells, we have a decreased
capacity to carry and

28
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,080
deliver oxygen and just
carry out everyday life.

29
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:30,440
So, the main players
with anemia are

30
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:33,200
known as erythrocytes,
red blood cells.

31
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:36,240
Their function, again,
is to carry oxygen and

32
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:39,860
carbon dioxide to the
peripheral vasculature and

33
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:43,000
then bring CO2 back to
the lungs for exchange.

34
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,660
Their shape is biconcave,
meaning that they have

35
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:50,060
a very nice middle area
that is kind of flattened

36
00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:51,940
and lower than the
rest of the blood cell.

37
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:55,080
They're also anucleate
cells, and they really don't

38
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:58,440
have any organelles within
the cell. This is all in an

39
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:01,520
effort to increase the amount
of cytoplasm in the cell.

40
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:03,920
The more cytoplasm
we have, the more

41
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:06,320
hemoglobin, and the
more oxygen-carrying

42
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:08,500
capacity each red
blood cell will have.

43
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:12,020
These cells are also
extremely flexible, which is

44
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:14,260
really important when
you consider they have to

45
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:18,280
navigate very small
capillaries in the peripheral

46
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:21,960
vasculature. And this
flexibility is actually due

47
00:02:21,960 --> 00:02:24,300
to special membrane
proteins, and we'll learn a

48
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:26,520
little bit more about this
in the following lecture.

49
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:29,380
And their lifespan
is about 120 days.

50
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:32,580
Even in healthy individuals,
you usually see about

51
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:36,040
1% of all red blood cells
turn over each and every

52
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:39,560
day. And they're simply
removed because they're old.

53
00:02:41,060 --> 00:02:43,640
So I want to talk a little
bit more about the life cycle

54
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:46,640
of a red blood cell. Red
blood cells are produced in

55
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:48,800
the bone marrow via a process
called erythropoiesis.

56
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:52,640
Erythropoietin growth
factor, also known as

57
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:56,140
EPO or EPO, helps
stimulate this process.

58
00:02:56,580 --> 00:02:59,540
The interstitial cells
in the peritubular

59
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:03,220
capillary of the renal
cortex sense a decrease in

60
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:05,780
O2 and actually secrete
the erythropoietin.

61
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:09,380
This EPO then signals
to the bone marrow to

62
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:11,760
actually start generating
new red blood cells.

63
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:14,900
And just a little fact,
for your knowledge,

64
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:17,540
erythropoietin and
hematocrit, which essentially

65
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:20,420
is the percentage of red
blood cells that comprise

66
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:23,340
your entire serum, are
inversely proportional.

67
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:25,740
Reticulocytes are very, very

68
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:27,660
important in this
entire process.

69
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:30,420
These are also known as
immature red blood cells

70
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:32,520
that are released into
circulation from the

71
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:36,380
bone marrow. And in about
24 to 48 hours from their

72
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:39,320
release, they will mature
into full red blood

73
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:42,420
cells. This reticulocyte
count in any normal

74
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:45,260
individual is usually
around one to two percent.

75
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:48,220
And on visualization
with the peripheral blood

76
00:03:48,220 --> 00:03:51,800
smear, these reticulocytes
appear more basophilic,

77
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:54,980
also known as a purplish
color, compared to

78
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:57,640
mature red blood cells.
And this is due to

79
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:00,760
ribosomal RNA still being
present in these cells.

80
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:04,600
And finally, once these
reticulocytes mature and they

81
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:08,060
go through about 120 days
of normal function as red

82
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:11,280
blood cells, these senescent
or now old red blood cells

83
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:14,420
are removed by macrophages
in the spleen, liver, and

84
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:16,930
bone marrow, and this
system is known as the

85
00:04:16,930 --> 00:04:20,560
reticuloendothelial system that
recycles the red blood cells.

86
00:04:28,030 --> 00:04:30,490
Generally, we can break
anemia down into three

87
00:04:30,490 --> 00:04:34,170
separate types of anemias.
All of these categories are

88
00:04:34,170 --> 00:04:36,750
fairly intuitive with the
pathophysiology behind

89
00:04:36,750 --> 00:04:40,510
them. The first one is simply
blood loss. We're losing

90
00:04:40,510 --> 00:04:43,890
more blood than we're able
to produce. Our production

91
00:04:43,890 --> 00:04:46,290
is not affected, but
we're simply losing more

92
00:04:46,290 --> 00:04:49,010
blood than the body can
maintain via new production.

93
00:04:50,070 --> 00:04:53,330
The second category is
actual decrease in red

94
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:55,830
blood cell production.
We have an issue with

95
00:04:55,830 --> 00:04:58,710
erythropoiesis. The body
cannot produce enough red

96
00:04:58,710 --> 00:05:01,330
blood cells to keep up
with the destruction, and

97
00:05:01,330 --> 00:05:03,910
we usually see a decreased
reticulocyte count.

98
00:05:03,910 --> 00:05:06,670
The bone marrow is not
responding properly to

99
00:05:06,670 --> 00:05:10,350
this new sense of anemia.
And finally, you could

100
00:05:10,350 --> 00:05:12,600
see almost the opposite
of decreased production,

101
00:05:12,810 --> 00:05:15,950
which is increased
destruction, also known as

102
00:05:15,950 --> 00:05:19,130
hemolysis. And this
is more destruction of

103
00:05:19,130 --> 00:05:21,250
red blood cells than
we actually can create.

104
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:24,030
And usually we'll see
splenomegaly and jaundice

105
00:05:24,030 --> 00:05:26,090
due to increased red
blood cell breakdown.

106
00:05:27,470 --> 00:05:30,870
So the clinical presentation
of anemia, there are

107
00:05:30,870 --> 00:05:33,730
vast signs and symptoms
that will usually vary with

108
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:37,030
the underlying etologies.
But all of these signs

109
00:05:37,030 --> 00:05:39,490
and symptoms can kind
of be pushed back to one

110
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:42,390
underlying reason. And that's
essentially the resulting

111
00:05:42,390 --> 00:05:46,910
tissue hypoxia due to
the decrease O2. And some

112
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:49,650
of these symptoms can
include weakness, fatigue,

113
00:05:49,650 --> 00:05:53,780
malaise, dyspnea on
mild exertion, some big

114
00:05:53,780 --> 00:05:56,410
buzzwords and things that
you may more commonly see in

115
00:05:56,410 --> 00:05:59,490
clinic are pallor, especially
in the conjunctiva.

116
00:05:59,490 --> 00:06:01,730
You may see pale
skin. You could

117
00:06:01,730 --> 00:06:03,570
see something
called spoon nails,

118
00:06:03,850 --> 00:06:06,250
dizziness, headache, faintness,

119
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:09,810
cardiac issues, cardiac
failure in some severe

120
00:06:09,810 --> 00:06:13,010
cases. and there are a
few specific anemias that

121
00:06:13,010 --> 00:06:15,430
may have specific
underlying pathologies,

122
00:06:15,430 --> 00:06:18,330
such as sickle cell
disease, along with sickle

123
00:06:18,330 --> 00:06:21,030
cell pain crisis, and
potentially renal failure.

124
00:06:21,030 --> 00:06:23,250
However, in general,
these symptoms

125
00:06:23,250 --> 00:06:25,610
are broad and don't
necessarily correlate

126
00:06:25,610 --> 00:06:27,830
with the severity
of the anemia.

127
00:06:27,910 --> 00:06:30,970
Essentially, what that
means is some individual

128
00:06:30,970 --> 00:06:33,730
may present with a very
severe anemia, but their

129
00:06:33,730 --> 00:06:36,810
symptoms may not present
similarly. They may just

130
00:06:36,810 --> 00:06:39,270
say, yeah, I feel tired
from time to time. then

131
00:06:39,270 --> 00:06:42,170
you look at their hemoglobin
levels and it's 7.2.

132
00:06:43,550 --> 00:06:47,170
So there are a few
measurements for anemia and

133
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:49,270
the first ones we
actually will look at are

134
00:06:49,270 --> 00:06:52,570
on every single CBC or
complete blood count.

135
00:06:53,030 --> 00:06:55,790
So these three values
are your red blood cell

136
00:06:55,790 --> 00:06:58,730
count, your hemoglobin,
and your hematocrit.

137
00:06:58,730 --> 00:07:01,150
And there's actually a
rule of three if we look at

138
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:04,390
these three values. For
normal, each of them should

139
00:07:04,390 --> 00:07:08,030
be 5, 15, and 45 respectfully.
So if you multiply

140
00:07:08,030 --> 00:07:10,830
the red blood cell count by
3, you get the hemoglobin.

141
00:07:10,950 --> 00:07:13,880
Multiply hemoglobin by
3, you get hematocrit.

142
00:07:14,030 --> 00:07:17,170
And just as a clinical
pearl, this isn't necessarily

143
00:07:17,170 --> 00:07:19,090
something that is important
for your education

144
00:07:19,090 --> 00:07:21,710
right now, but it will be
in two years when you're

145
00:07:21,710 --> 00:07:25,190
in the wards on rotations,
is that one unit of

146
00:07:25,190 --> 00:07:28,110
packed red blood cells will
increase your hemoglobin

147
00:07:28,110 --> 00:07:31,770
by 1 and your hematocrit
by 3. And again, we see

148
00:07:31,770 --> 00:07:36,170
this rule of 3 being in
play here. 1 times 3 is 3.

149
00:07:38,090 --> 00:07:40,010
Additionally, just
to kind of continue

150
00:07:40,010 --> 00:07:41,470
to talk about
these measurements,

151
00:07:41,470 --> 00:07:44,350
the red blood cell count
is the actual number of red

152
00:07:44,350 --> 00:07:47,510
blood cells in the blood.
The hemoglobin is the

153
00:07:47,510 --> 00:07:50,470
concentration of hemoglobin
in the blood, and it actually

154
00:07:50,470 --> 00:07:53,450
reflects a red blood cell's
ability to carry oxygen.

155
00:07:53,630 --> 00:07:56,090
And finally, hematocrit
measures the percentage

156
00:07:56,090 --> 00:07:58,430
of erythrocyte volume
in the blood sample.

157
00:07:58,430 --> 00:08:01,010
This essentially shows
what percentage of the

158
00:08:01,010 --> 00:08:04,190
entire whole blood is
actually red blood cells.

159
00:08:04,330 --> 00:08:07,490
And importantly, in
patients with anemia, they

160
00:08:07,490 --> 00:08:10,810
typically have low hemoglobin
and hematocrit, and

161
00:08:10,810 --> 00:08:13,630
you should also see low
red blood cell counts.

162
00:08:16,450 --> 00:08:19,150
So, hemoglobin. You've heard
me reference it a couple

163
00:08:19,150 --> 00:08:22,430
times, but let's actually
describe what it is. Hemoglobin

164
00:08:22,430 --> 00:08:24,730
is the oxygen-carrying
aspect of the red blood

165
00:08:24,730 --> 00:08:27,870
cell. It's almost the star of
the show without hemoglobin

166
00:08:27,870 --> 00:08:30,890
red blood cells are fairly
useless and it's composed

167
00:08:30,890 --> 00:08:35,790
of two very self-explanatory
parts heme which is actually

168
00:08:35,790 --> 00:08:38,890
made up of iron and
protoporphyrin and then globin

169
00:08:38,890 --> 00:08:42,730
which are protein chains
typically in a normal hemoglobin

170
00:08:42,730 --> 00:08:46,310
which is hba you will
have two alpha chains and

171
00:08:46,310 --> 00:08:50,150
then two beta chains and as
you can see here the heme

172
00:08:50,150 --> 00:08:53,870
is actually comprised of
protoporphyrin and then iron.

173
00:08:56,330 --> 00:08:59,390
So there are additional
measurements that we can use

174
00:08:59,390 --> 00:09:02,330
to help identify specific
anemias and then also start

175
00:09:02,330 --> 00:09:05,790
to classify them. The
first value in a very very

176
00:09:05,790 --> 00:09:08,810
key one is something called
mean cell volume, which is

177
00:09:08,810 --> 00:09:11,530
essentially the average
size of the red blood cell.

178
00:09:12,390 --> 00:09:15,110
Another key value that
kind of goes hand-in-hand

179
00:09:15,110 --> 00:09:17,390
with the size of the red
blood cell is the red

180
00:09:17,390 --> 00:09:20,170
blood cell distribution
width. And this actually

181
00:09:20,170 --> 00:09:23,870
measures the variability
of the MCV. And what that

182
00:09:23,870 --> 00:09:26,650
means is it looks at all
the red blood cells in

183
00:09:26,650 --> 00:09:30,850
a CBC and sees what the
range of sizes is. So if

184
00:09:30,850 --> 00:09:34,050
all the cells are a
similar size, then they'll

185
00:09:34,050 --> 00:09:38,290
have a low variation, low
variance, and a low RDW.

186
00:09:38,530 --> 00:09:41,510
However, if we have
cells of many different

187
00:09:41,510 --> 00:09:44,350
sizes, some are big,
some red blood cells are

188
00:09:44,350 --> 00:09:47,590
small, some red blood
cells or normal size,

189
00:09:47,590 --> 00:09:50,230
we will see a higher
RDW because we have a

190
00:09:50,230 --> 00:09:53,810
larger range or larger
variance in the size.

191
00:09:54,190 --> 00:09:57,190
To measure the actual
amount of hemoglobin per red

192
00:09:57,190 --> 00:10:01,010
blood cell, we can look at two
values, the mean corpuscular

193
00:10:01,010 --> 00:10:03,670
hemoglobin, which is the
amount of hemoglobin in

194
00:10:03,670 --> 00:10:06,710
an average erythrocyte,
and the mean corpuscular

195
00:10:06,710 --> 00:10:10,050
hemoglobin concentration, which
provides the percentage of

196
00:10:10,050 --> 00:10:13,430
hemoglobin concentration
in an average erythrocyte.

197
00:10:13,570 --> 00:10:15,910
And finally, something
you've already heard me

198
00:10:15,910 --> 00:10:19,390
reference once or twice is
the reticulocyte count, which

199
00:10:19,390 --> 00:10:22,170
is the percentage of total
red blood cells that are

200
00:10:22,170 --> 00:10:25,570
reticulocytes. This is really
important in determining

201
00:10:25,570 --> 00:10:29,450
when we see an anemia
if the underlying cause

202
00:10:29,450 --> 00:10:31,790
could be the bone marrow
or if the bone marrow is

203
00:10:31,790 --> 00:10:35,610
responding appropriately to
the anemia. And what I mean

204
00:10:35,610 --> 00:10:39,770
by that is that when we
have an anemia and the bone

205
00:10:39,770 --> 00:10:42,710
marrow is signaled from
erythropoietin to start

206
00:10:42,710 --> 00:10:45,690
generating more red blood
cells, you would expect to see

207
00:10:45,690 --> 00:10:48,890
more reticulocytes or a
higher reticulocyte count.

208
00:10:49,350 --> 00:10:52,390
And if we see this increased
reticulocyte count,

209
00:10:52,390 --> 00:10:54,630
we know the marrow is
responding appropriately.

210
00:10:54,630 --> 00:10:57,770
It recognizes the anemia
and is trying to push

211
00:10:57,770 --> 00:11:01,590
more red blood cells out
to correct for it. If we

212
00:11:01,590 --> 00:11:04,770
have an anemia without this
increase in reticulocyte

213
00:11:04,770 --> 00:11:06,970
count, it actually
could point to the bone

214
00:11:06,970 --> 00:11:10,410
marrow being the underlying
issue for the anemia.

215
00:11:10,550 --> 00:11:13,890
But just one little caveat
with this, remember that

216
00:11:13,890 --> 00:11:16,790
like most other things in
the body there can be a

217
00:11:16,790 --> 00:11:20,170
delay so the bone marrow
takes some time to respond

218
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:25,290
so after we do some of our
lab tests we can actually

219
00:11:25,290 --> 00:11:28,150
now look and visualize the
red blood cells themselves

220
00:11:28,150 --> 00:11:30,630
and it's a follow-up
test that we could do for

221
00:11:30,630 --> 00:11:33,650
an abnormal cpc and it's
called a peripheral blood

222
00:11:33,650 --> 00:11:36,470
smear and this is where we
actually visually inspect

223
00:11:36,470 --> 00:11:39,120
the red blood cells for
their shape and their color

224
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:42,310
and you use a special
stain in this peripheral

225
00:11:42,310 --> 00:11:44,810
blood smear and it's known
as the right gasmus stain.

226
00:11:45,170 --> 00:11:48,170
When we examine a normal
peripheral blood smear,

227
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:50,630
you want to notice a few
different things, and the

228
00:11:50,630 --> 00:11:53,030
most important being this
almost central area of

229
00:11:53,030 --> 00:11:56,130
pallor that you can see.
And this is actually due

230
00:11:56,130 --> 00:11:59,610
to the biconcave disc shape
of the red blood cell.

231
00:11:59,810 --> 00:12:03,090
What I mean by that is,
if you can remember, that

232
00:12:03,090 --> 00:12:06,790
red blood cell almost has
a, and I'm going to take out

233
00:12:06,790 --> 00:12:09,930
my pen, my apologies for
my poor drawing skills,

234
00:12:11,830 --> 00:12:20,370
have a kind of disc shape in
the cell, and it's actually

235
00:12:20,370 --> 00:12:22,970
in this middle piece,
you have less cytoplasm,

236
00:12:22,970 --> 00:12:25,690
right? We just have less
volume in this middle area.

237
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:28,430
Because we have less
cytoplasm in this

